ual, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "13 – 14 October 1964"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/456"} "Memorial Hall, Waseda Daigaku, Shinjuku, Tokyo"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/90541"} "55 from 21 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Although he had won a bronze medal at the previous year’s World Championships, " [:a {:href "/athletes/23065"} "Egon Franke"] " of Poland was considered no more than a fringe contender for an Olympic medal and not even his country’s best chance for the gold – that honour belonged to ex-World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/23110"} "Ryszard Parulski"] ". Franke was not especially impressive in the qualifying stages but did enough to reach the final four alongside French World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/21947"} "Jean-Claude Magnan"] ", another Frenchman in the form of " [:a {:href "/athletes/22021"} "Daniel Revenu"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/21183"} "Roland Losert"] " of Austria. The Pole defeated the two Frenchmen, both by 5-4 and then also beat Losert convincingly to become the first Olympic fencing champion from Poland. Magnan defeated Revenu to claim silver. Franke did not defend his title in 1968 although he did compete and win a medal in the team event. He later married fellow Olympic fencer " [:a {:href "/athletes/20866"} "Elżbieta Cymerman"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "190"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/23065"} "Egon Franke"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/POL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/POL.png"}] "POL"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold